  "llm_insights": {
    "narrative": "# Your 2025 Outside Memory: A Year with Robin\n\n## Welcome Message\n\nWelcome, Robin! We're thrilled to celebrate your incredible year of engagement with Outside Online and our community. With nearly **22,000 interactions** across our platforms over the past year, you've been an essential part of our community\u2014diving deep into cycling news, exploring trails, and staying connected to the outdoor lifestyle. This is your personalized story.\n\n---\n\n## Robin's 2025 Outside Memory\n\nYour year began modestly in late January, but by June, something shifted. The cycling world captured your attention in a way that would define the next three months of your life. As summer arrived, your engagement exploded\u2014June brought 2,293 events, and July pushed even higher with 2,625. But August? August was your peak: a staggering **4,574 events** as the Tour de France dominated your feed and your focus.\n\nThis wasn't casual browsing. You were *living* the 2025 cycling season. You followed every development of Tadej Poga\u010dar's dominance (178 tags), Jonas Vingegaard's comeback story (143 tags), and the drama unfolding at Visma-Lease a Bike. You read Jim Cotton's race analysis, followed Andrew Hood's coverage of the chaos at Volta ao Algarve, and caught Shane Stokes' insights into the sport's biggest moments. The Tour de France alone generated 200 tagged interactions\u2014you didn't miss a stage.\n\nBut you're not a one-sport enthusiast. September remained strong at 3,790 events as the cycling calendar continued, but by autumn, your engagement naturally moderated. October still showed solid interest at 2,979 events, but the intensity of summer had passed. Winter brought a quieter rhythm\u20141,449 events in November, 1,418 in December\u2014as you shifted your attention and perhaps your activities outdoors. Your profile shows you're not just reading about adventures; you're *having* them. Your activity posts reveal ski days at Winter Park, Zwift rides, and bike builds in progress.\n\nThroughout the year, you've been a map explorer and trail enthusiast too. Trailforks and Gaia GPS kept you connected to the ground-level outdoor experience, while your consistent engagement with your profile and the community dashboard shows you're deeply invested in the social side of the outdoor world.\n\n---\n\n## Your Passions\n\n- **Professional Road Cycling** \u2014 With 200 Tour de France tags, 178 dedicated to Tadej Poga\u010dar, and deep dives into teams like Visma-Lease a Bike (55 tags) and riders like Jonas Vingegaard, Remco Evenepoel, and Mathieu van der Poel, cycling is clearly your primary obsession. You follow the sport with genuine expertise.\n\n- **Trail Riding & Mountain Biking** \u2014 Your 2,211 Trailforks interactions and consistent engagement with trail maps, ride logs, and the Trailforks community show you're an active trail rider. You're not just planning routes; you're logging rides and connecting with other riders.\n\n- **Winter Sports & Skiing** \u2014 Your shared activities include epic ski days at Winter Park, and your engagement with avalanche news and winter-specific content reveals you're actively pursuing snow-based adventures, not just reading about them.\n\n- **Outdoor Exploration & Public Lands** \u2014 With 65 tags on public lands, 59 on national parks, and 45 specifically on Yosemite, you care deeply about where you adventure. You're an explorer who appreciates the places that make outdoor life possible.\n\n- **Gear & Design** \u2014 Your 47 interactions with design-and-tech content and 43 tags each on footwear and sandals reveal you're interested in the equipment that enables your adventures. Your activity post about building a new bike shows you engage hands-on with gear.\n\n- **Wellness & Science** \u2014 Beyond the action sports, you've explored health topics ranging from sex differences in sport to longevity experiments to microplastics\u2014suggesting you think holistically about outdoor performance and wellbeing.\n\n---\n\n## Reading Habits\n\nYou're a **deliberate, strategic reader** with clear patterns. Your engagement peaks during weekday afternoons and evenings\u20141 PM (1,787 events), 6 PM (1,807 events), and 8 PM (1,438 events) are your prime reading windows. You're checking in during work breaks and evening wind-downs, not random scrolling. Mondays through Thursdays are your heaviest engagement days (3,969, 3,677, 3,723, and 3,358 events respectively), suggesting you're using Outside content to fuel your weekday routine.\n\nYour author preferences reveal you value **authoritative, narrative-driven cycling journalism**. Jim Cotton (61 articles read) is your go-to for race coverage, followed closely by Andrew Hood (53 articles) for dynamic, on-the-ground reporting\u2014like his coverage of the Volta ao Algarve chaos. Shane Stokes (36 articles) rounds out your top three, bringing analytical depth to race narratives. You've also engaged with Frederick Dreier, Will Tracy, and Alex Hutchinson, suggesting you appreciate both hard news and deeper analytical pieces.\n\nYour reading journey across 2025 tells a story of seasonal interests. Winter and spring (January through May) were exploratory\u2014you sampled content across VeloNews, Outside Online, and trail platforms, averaging just 75-541 events per month. But the moment the cycling calendar heated up in June, you committed fully. You weren't just reading headlines; you were consuming 964 unique articles across 95 different authors, engaging with 200 distinct tags, and building a comprehensive understanding of professional cycling's biggest season. Your engagement data shows you're not a skimmer\u2014you're a *student* of the sport and the outdoor world.",
    "recent_spotlight": "**Logging Your Adventures: You're All About the Ride Details**\n\nYou've been diving deep into Trailforks this week, spending quality time reviewing and perfecting your ride logs. Whether you're editing activity details, checking out your personal ride history, or browsing the community activity feed, you're clearly committed to keeping your trail experiences documented and organized. It looks like you're in the thick of activity management mode!\n\nYour recent focus areas include: Activity Edit pages, your personal ride logs collection, ride log info details, and the Trailforks.com Activity Feed. Keep up the momentum\u2014your trail community is probably loving seeing your latest adventures!",
